module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Processes

let content () : String =
  """# Processes: things run at once, pause, and resume

Every trace is a process the runtime owns: it can run beside others, be paused,
looked at, stopped, and picked up again later. You mostly meet this without
writing anything.

## Concurrency, without writing anything

Reads run at the same time. Writes keep program order. `await x` waits for a
value here instead of wherever it is first used.

  let pages = Stdlib.List.map urls (fun u -> Stdlib.HttpClient.get u [])  // all in flight
  Stdlib.printLine "fetching"                                             // a write: now, in order
  let count = Stdlib.List.length pages                                    // looks: waits for all

A read is a call whose effects are all reads (a file, env, db or package
read, an HTTP GET or HEAD). Anything else runs in order.

## When you want control

  let h = Stdlib.Exec.spawn (fun () -> Stdlib.Cli.File.writeText log "done")  // in the background
  Stdlib.Exec.await h                                    // wait for it
  Stdlib.Exec.awaitWithin 500L h                         // ... or give up after 500 ms: None
  Stdlib.Exec.select [ h1; h2 ]                          // the first to finish
  Stdlib.Exec.cancel h                                   // stop it, and its children
  Stdlib.List.parallelMap items fib                      // CPU-bound work across the cores

A process spawned this way dies with its parent unless `Exec.spawnDetached`.
Spawning is the `concurrency` effect; a policy can forbid it.

## See what is running

  ps                     every Dark process on this machine, then this one's own tree
  ps --watch             the same, repainting; up/down, c cancel, k kill, esc
  ps show <id> [--full]  one process: status, instructions, memory, call stack
  ps cancel <id> | <pid> stop it politely (what it has on the host finishes first)
  ps kill <id> | <pid>   stop it now (for a process stuck in a call that never returns)

A tight loop cannot freeze a TUI or a server: it gets a turn, then others
run. In a system monitor a Dark process shows as what it runs: `dark serve
:9090`, `dark eval`, `dark <daemon>`.

## Read your code through a run, and pick one back up

Every `eval`, every `run` and every served request is kept as a trace (only its
effects: what it read, wrote, sent and got back), so you can look at what
happened, and so Ctrl-C does not lose it.

The one to learn first is `show`. It takes a FUNCTION, not an id, and uses that
function's newest run, so there is nothing to look up:

  traces show <fn> [<id>]       that function's code, with a run's values beside
                                each line, what the run was given, and what this
                                time round the loop was given
  traces show <fn> --iteration map:2
                                a particular time round a loop. Recursion counts
                                as a loop, and so does a function a loop called
                                once per iteration
  traces show <fn> --watch      the same as a panel, repainting as runs arrive
                                and as you edit the code
  traces show <fn> --json       the same for an agent, `--iteration` included

A `show` RE-RUNS the function, answering every effect from the log and performing
none, so the values are recomputed rather than stored. That means the page shows
the code as it is NOW, which is what makes it useful while you edit, and it also
means a replay can stop on something the recorded run sailed through: a callee
deleted since, a name that moved, something deprecated as harmful. The page says
which of the two happened, because they want opposite next steps:

  the replay stopped here: <error>        the code raised. Debug it
  the replay stopped: the store has ...   it ran once and the world moved. The
                                          page then names `traces inspect <id>`,
                                          which reads the log and re-runs
                                          nothing, so it still has what the run
                                          answered

`--json` carries the same judgement as `problem.drift`, so an agent does not have
to read the prose to tell them apart.

A view holds the first five times round each loop and the last five, and goes
and gets any other one you ask for. So nothing in a loop is out of reach however
long it ran, and a two-thousand-iteration loop costs the same to open as a short
one. The page always shows ONE iteration at a time and says which: several
values on one line are several answers to one question.

Finding your way to a function, from either direction:

  traces calls <fn>             the runs that went through one function
  traces fns [n]                ... and the other way: which functions recent
                                runs went through, most first
  dark view <fn>                ends with how many runs went through it

And the rest:

  traces                        the traces kept here, newest first
  traces inspect <id>           one trace: what it ran, where it stands, what
                                it did to the world, in order
  traces resume <id>            carry on where it stopped: the log answers what
                                already happened, then the trace goes live
  traces fork <id> [--at <n>]   a new trace from any point of an old one
  traces pin <id>               keep it, whatever else is dropped to make room

A resume echoes what the old trace printed (dimmed), stops if the log holds
something it cannot have again (an OS subprocess, an open stream), and warns
when a file it read has changed since. The oldest are dropped once there are
more than `trace.keep` traces (a served request is one) or `trace.maxMb` of
log; a suspended trace's never.

What the log holds: what each effect was given and returned. Secret request
headers (`authorization`, `cookie`, `set-cookie`, `x-api-key`) are stored as
`[redacted]`, and an environment read's value is not stored at all (a resume
reads the environment again). Everything else is in the clear: a key file a run
read, a response body, what it printed.

What the log does NOT hold: the value of anything pure. `traces show` does not
read values off a disk, it RE-RUNS the function with every effectful call
answered from the log, collecting each expression's value as it goes. Three
things follow from that, and they surprise people in both directions. Values
follow an edit, so fixing a callee and looking again shows the new numbers
against the old recorded effects, with no new run. A function that did nothing
impure still has values, because they are computed rather than stored. And
arithmetic is shown for the same reason everything else is: the replay watches
it happen.

How a replay knows which expression an instruction belongs to: each package
function carries a table of it, built when the code was compiled and stored
beside the instructions. It is read only when something replays, so an ordinary
run never pays for it, and it is about 3% of what the code itself takes.

Which runs count as "this function's" is asked two ways, because each alone is
wrong. Dark is content-addressed, so two byte-identical functions in different
modules are ONE function and both names find the same runs. But editing anything
a function calls changes its hash too, so by hash alone a view would go blank
the moment you edited anything. Both: the hash finds runs of this CODE under any
name, the name finds runs of this FUNCTION under any code.

Nothing is recorded until you ask for it. Three ways: `dark traces record on`
for this instance, `config set trace.record on` for the same setting by the
other door, and `dark --trace <command>` for one command (`--no-trace` for the
opposite).

## Knobs, all optional

  config set trace.record on|off       keep a record of every trace (default: off)
  config set exec.workers <n>          worker threads (default: one per core)
  config set http.requestTimeoutMs <n> a server cancels a handler past this: 504 (30 s)
  config set trace.keep <n>            traces kept (200); trace.maxMb, MB of log kept (256)
  config set exec.maxInstructions <n>  expert: stop a process past this many instructions
  config set exec.maxBytes <n>         expert: ... or this many bytes allocated

The design is `docs/processes.md` in the repository; the live half of the
story is `docs live`.
"""
